.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1039 PM PDT Mon Aug 31 2026
(Wednesday night through next Monday)

Rain chances peak Thursday morning with an 90% chance of
precipitation in the North Bay, and a 70% chance of at least
1/10" at Santa Rosa. Again the rain chances fall off quickly as
the system moves south, but there`s a decent chance (20-50%) for
lighter rain across the cwa. The cooler weather will continue
through Friday as the trough produces a cut-off low near the
Pacific Northwest. By the weekend, high pressure will build over
the desert SW. The interaction between the low pressure to the NW
and high pressure to the SE brings more uncertainty to the
forecast. If the trough is dominant the cooler weather will stick
around, if not then we can expect a warming trend this weekend.

The National Hurricane Center continues to track Invest 95E in the
Eastern Pacific. While there is now a 100% chance of tropical
cyclone formation over the next 48 hours, the circulation center
was displaced from the thunderstorm activity and it struggled to
get organized today. The eventual track of this system is still
uncertain, with the ensembles showing a bi-modal solution. One of
these outcomes would bring wind and rain to Southern California
around Sunday or Monday. Either the PNW cut-off low will move far
enough south to pull the decaying tropical cyclone towards
Southern California, or high pressure will build south of the low,
forcing the storm harmlessly to the NW. While there is a lot of
hype around different deterministic solutions, it`s much more
appropriate to look at ensembles in this time range. Both the GEFS
and ECMWF ensemble show a 20-30% chance of tropical depression
level impacts in Southern California, and a 10-20% chance of
tropical storm type impacts. Even in the worst case scenario, our
cwa wouldn`t get nearly as much rain or wind as Southern
California. The current high end scenario (90th percentile) would
bring 1/4" of rain to the Central Coast. The most likely outcome
is no significant impacts, and the ensembles have trended more in
that direction over the last 24 hours. There is virtually no
chance for hurricane impacts in California. It`s worth watching if
another system forms behind 95E, as persistent troughing could
keep the door open through next week.

